WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.312
This query partitions website visits by user and orders them by visit time and visit ID.

00:00:05.312 --> 00:00:07.932
LEAD retrieves the next visit timestamp.

00:00:07.932 --> 00:00:11.457
Subtracting the current timestamp gives time to the next visit.

00:00:11.457 --> 00:00:17.177
The last visit in each user sequence has no next row, so LEAD returns NULL.

00:00:17.177 --> 00:00:21.972
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:21.972 --> 00:00:25.837
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:25.837 --> 00:00:29.717
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:29.717 --> 00:00:34.977
In a real setting such as a sales team calculating running revenue and top products by region,

00:00:34.977 --> 00:00:39.622
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:39.622 --> 00:00:42.212
before discussing syntax.

00:00:42.212 --> 00:00:47.920
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
